WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.430
(peaceful music)

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:13.850
- When I was a kid
looking up at the stars,

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:16.580
I really always wondered how did we get

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:20.163
here and are we alone?

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.370
My name is Bethany Ehlmann.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:27.120
I'm a professor of
planetary science at Caltech

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.780
and Research Scientist at the NASA

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.223
Jet Propulsion Laboratory.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.740
I remember when I was six,
I declared that I wanted

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.400
to be an astrophysicist
like Steven Hawking.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.480
And I'm not an astrophysicist,
but I am an explorer

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.600
an explorer of planets of asteroids,

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.220
and I really enjoy the
unknown in space exploration.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.200
We're going places that really,

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.620
no one has gone before.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.410
And it's one of the few types of science

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:54.510
where you can do that.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.850
Our solar system is about four
and a half billion years old

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.970
and asteroids formed within
the first few million years

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:07.180
that the solar system existed.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.300
Asteroids are mostly
metal and rock and ice,

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.293
and are thought to be the
shattered remnants of planets.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:24.600
They're these bank vaults
full of information

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.520
about what conditions were
like early in the solar system

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:28.953
as planets were forming.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:35.170
What we're looking at here
is the largest asteroid

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.660
in the asteroid belt
and we're able to have

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:41.320
this amazing view because of
the Dawn spacecraft mission,

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.453
which orbited Ceres for several years.

00:01:46.863 --> 00:01:49.760
- [Announcer] Main
engine start, one, zero,

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.390
and lift off of the
Delta II rocket lift off.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:57.480
- The Dawn mission was a
spacecraft that was launched

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.440
in 2007 and it was
designed to study Ceres,

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.620
which is the largest asteroid
in the asteroid belt.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:04.220
And, it's actually not just an asteroid.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:05.455
It's a dwarf planet.

00:02:05.455 --> 00:02:08.038
(upbeat music)

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:18.040
One of the most fascinating
things about Ceres

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:21.930
that we discovered is it
wasn't a uniform grey.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:25.670
Some parts of the surface had
these amazing brighter spots.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:30.113
Here you can see one of them
coming out of the crater.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.313
This was totally unexpected.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.470
One of the fun parts of
being a planetary scientist,

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:46.410
and especially my job, is
that I have a laboratory

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:50.360
where we get to measure the
spectroscopic signatures

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:51.923
of rocks and minerals.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:56.810
Every material has a characteristic way

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.040
that it reflects light.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.290
We can call this a spectral signature.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:03.460
And so one of the things that
we can do is here on Earth,

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:06.890
we can measure the spectral
signatures of all sorts

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:09.700
of materials that we know
exactly what they are.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:12.090
And then we can compare those signatures

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.290
to the reflected light
we get from asteroids

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:16.540
to figure out what those
objects are made of.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:21.500
Now on Ceres we basically
repeat the measurement,

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.170
but our spacecraft is the sensor,

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:24.830
instead of my lab instrument.

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:26.980
And the source, instead of a halogen light

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.380
in the lab, is the sun.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:32.850
And so by comparing those materials

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.290
in the lab to the materials
the we see on other planets,

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:38.190
that's how we know what planets
and asteroids are made of.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.890
When we looked at the data, we found

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.550
that these bright spots
were actually made of salts,

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:51.520
and these salts were being
pulled up by impacts.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.770
So, salts, underneath
the surface of Ceres?

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:55.720
What did that mean?

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:00.480
That meant that there were salty waters

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.120
underneath Ceres at some period of time.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.653
Maybe even a salty
ocean, deep underground.

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:13.810
What past underground
waters tells us on Ceres

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:15.650
is that Ceres was once wet.

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:20.119
And because other asteroids
are like Ceres, water rich,

00:04:20.119 --> 00:04:22.570
that tells us that some of the asteroids

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:25.420
that were hitting Earth
early in its history

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:27.590
were probably delivering some of the water

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.313
that's found on our planet today.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.500
One of the key questions about asteroids

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:37.613
is did the play any role
in the origin of life?

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:43.050
Asteroids brought water and carbon,

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:45.740
some of the most important
ingredients for life,

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:48.303
to our planet very early in its history.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.980
Did they help make earth this amazing

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.100
habitable place it is today?

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.340
We don't fully know the answer.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:57.300
We think they played
a role, but that's one

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:00.884
of things were trying really
hard to figure out right now.

00:05:00.884 --> 00:05:03.634
(majestic music)

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:15.140
So these are some of the
reasons that I explore,

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:18.520
is curiosity and a desire to learn things

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:20.123
that no one yet knows.

